Subject: Close each open block with a MISSING brace at the end of the input

Close each open block with a MISSING brace at the end of the input.

The copy of the runtime of the tree-sitter-cpp fork has this repair. The fork commit is
93d3bcd.

At the end of the input, error recovery put a MISSING token only when
the end of the input could follow that one token. With two unclosed
blocks, as in `void f() {\n g();\n if (x) {\n h();\n`, no single token
could close the input. Recovery then put the function into one ERROR
node. For each unclosed block, GCC reports "expected '}' at end of
input" (cp_parser_require in gcc/cp/parser.cc), and Clang reports
"expected '}'" (Parser::ExpectAndConsume in clang/lib/Parse/Parser.cpp).
The two compilers keep the function and the if statement.

When no single MISSING token is possible at the end of the input,
ts_parser__insert_missing_braces uses a copy of the version. It does the
reductions for a `}` lookahead and pushes a MISSING `}`, again until the
end of the input has a reduce action. Each `}` must have a reduce action
for the end of the input or for one more `}`. The copy stays only when
the sequence gets to such a state. The reductions of the copy do not
merge with other versions. A sequence that stops then leaves the other
versions as they were.

On the corpus at f0adac0, 3 files that had an ERROR node have MISSING
braces and no ERROR node, and the ERROR bytes go from 8,056,059 to
7,939,734. No file without an error changes. A benchmark puts one error
into each of 3,997 files without an error. In it, the ERROR bytes go from
2,210,618 to 1,961,378. For the edits that add a line with a brace, they
go from 504,750 to 337,171.

A class body at the end of the input also needs a `;` after its `}`, and
this sequence does not close it.

The repair reads the names of the token symbols to find `}` and `;`. Upstream can reject a
heuristic that reads token names in the runtime of each language. An alternative gives the
symbols of the missing tokens in the language.

Risk for upstream: high, because of the point above.

The patch applies to the upstream tag v0.27.0, after the patches with a smaller number. The
series compiles with `-Wall -Wextra` and no warning.

The comments hold the marker "(tree-sitter-cpp fork)". Remove the marker before you send the
patch upstream.

